# Machine output — never committed (unanchored: applies in every folder)
node_modules/
.next/
out/

# Local secrets + OS noise
.env*
# ...except the TEMPLATE. `.env*` correctly keeps every real value out of git, and
# for a while it kept the list of WHICH values exist out too — which is the half
# that cannot be reissued from a provider's dashboard. `.env.example` holds names
# and comments, never a value; the negation is anchored to that one filename so
# nothing else can slip back in behind it.
!.env.example
.DS_Store
*.log
*.tsbuildinfo

# Worker local secrets — keep out of git
.dev.vars
.wrangler/
.dev-state/

# Skill output — regenerable, never source. Each of these is written into the
# repo ROOT by a skill that re-runs on demand, which is exactly where canon
# lives, so an old run reads like a document someone wrote on purpose. The
# lean-mean and interface-lessness reports were deleted by hand once for that
# reason; ignoring them is what stops the next run putting them back.
/architecture-blueprint.html
/architecture-review.html
/architecture-review.md
/lean-mean-report.html
/lean-mean-report.md
/interface-lessness-report.html
/interface-lessness-report.md
/story-review.html
/story-review.md
/ocean-review.html
/ocean-review.md
# NOT scaling-review.md/.html. README's doc map lists it as item 27 and
# DATA-MODEL.md cites it for a claim, so it is a DOCUMENT that two other
# documents depend on — ignoring it left the map pointing at a file a fresh
# clone does not have.

# WHERE scripts/backup.mjs PUTS THE BACKUPS, and the one line standing between a
# routine `git add -A` and every client's database in the repository. The script
# defaults to ./backups and the runbooks tell you to run it from the repo root,
# so without this the dumps land INSIDE the working tree — real customer rows,
# staged by the next commit that adds everything. The architecture review flagged
# it as the gap it would not close in a contended file; this is the file.
/backups/

# Per-workstream working notes — local to whoever is building, never forked
# into a new app (the new-app skill clones this repo).
.session-notes/

# The pulled Glide rows are real customer data — the catalogue is tracked, the
# data never is. Re-pull with `node scripts/glide-pull.mjs`.
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, on this and on the files rule below. A trailing slash means
# "a directory", and a SYMLINK to the data is not one — so `glide/data/` left a
# symlink named `glide/data` untracked-but-not-ignored, which is one `git add -A`
# away from committing the path to a client's whole export. A worktree is exactly
# where somebody reaches for that symlink, because the data lives in one checkout
# and the work happens in another.
glide/data
# The rescued Glide FILES — 79 MB of the clients' own logos, photos, PDFs and
# video, copied off Google's storage before the Glide account lapses. Same rule
# as the rows: the SCRIPTS that fetch and shape them are tracked, their output
# never is. `glide/files/manifest.json` maps every file back to the row that
# referenced it. BACK THIS UP SOMEWHERE REAL — once the Glide account is closed,
# re-running `node scripts/glide-files.mjs` gets nothing.
glide/files
# Derived from both, so it holds exactly the same customer data.
glide/normalised.json
# Where each rescued file now lives in OUR R2, and the row that referenced it —
# written by `node scripts/glide-to-r2.mjs <staging|production>`, and the map the
# migration will read to repoint those rows. Same customer data, one more shape.
glide/r2-manifest.json

# Parallel agent worktrees — working copies, never content
.claude/worktrees/

# The retrieval benchmark's corpus and its cached vectors — hundreds of MB,
# rebuildable by scripts/knowledge-retrieval-bench.mjs.
.kb-bench/

# Claude Code skills live globally in ~/.claude/skills/ — never vendored here
/skills/
